  Alerting Process and Analysis
    Detection rules are pretty much logical expressions set to be triggered.

  How to create detection rule
    Example use case: 
      Adversaries use commands like whoami after the exploitation/privilege escalation phase. The following Fields will be helpful to include in the rule.
      Log source: Identify the log source capturing the event logs
      Event ID: Which Event ID is associated with Process Execution activity? In this case, Event ID 4688 will be helpful.
      NewProcessName: Which process name will be helpful to include in the rule?

      Rule: If Log Source is WinEventLog AND EventCode is 4688, and NewProcessName contains whoami, then Trigger an ALERT WHOAMI command Execution DETECTED

  Alert investigation
  Once an alert is triggered, the events/flows associated with the alert are examined, and the rule is checked to see which conditions are met
  Some of the actions that are performed after the analysis are:
    Alert is a False Positive. It may require tuning the rule to avoid similar False positives from occurring again.
    Alert is a True Positive. Perform further investigation.
    Contact the asset owner to inquire about the activity.
    Suspicious activity is confirmed. (Actions like Isolate, Block etc.)

IDS (Intrusion Detection System)
An IDS’s main categorization depends on its deployment and detection modes.

  Deployment modes
    HIDS:
      installed individually on hosts
      provide visibility of host activities
      challenging to manage in large networks as they are resource intensive and require management on each host

    NIDS:
      detect malicious activities within the network, regardless of specific hosts
      monitor network traffic of all the hosts involved to detect suspicious activities
      provides centralized view of all detections inside the whole network

  Detection Modes
    Signature based:
      Each attack has its unique pattern known as signature. These signatures are preserved by the IDS in their database
      unable to detect zero-day attacks (have no prior signatures)

    Anomaly based
      learns the normal behavior (baseline) of network or system
      performs detections if there is any deviation
      Can detect zero day
      may generate a lot of false positives (can be reduced by mmanually defining the normal behavior)

    Hybrid
      combines signature based and anomaly based to leverage strength of each approach
      
SNORT
  Open source
  use signature based and anomaly based detectoins
  defined in rule files
  comes with built-in rules files preinstalled in tool package
  custom rules can be created and built in rules can be disabled

  Modes of snort:
    packet sniffer:
      reads and displays network packets without performing analysis
      main use case is network monitoring, analysis and diagnostics

    packet logging
      allows logging of network traffic as PCAP (standard packet capture format)
      used to perform the root cause analysis

    NIDS
      monitors traffic in real time and applies rule files to identify anu known attack patterns
      used to monitor systems/network to detect potential threats

